/-
# Finite presentability of the Andrews-Curtis groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 18.89)

Kourovka Notebook, Problem 18.89 (J. Swan, A. Lisitsa), marked solved in the 21st issue: consider
the balanced presentations `⟨x₁, …, xₙ | r₁, …, rₙ⟩` with fixed generators. The group `ACₙ` of
Andrews-Curtis moves on this set of balanced presentations is generated by the Nielsen
transformations together with conjugations of relators. Is `ACₙ` finitely presented?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.GroupTheory.FinitelyPresentedGroup

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.AndrewsCurtisGroupPresentationKourovka18Problem89Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 18.89 (J. Swan, A. Lisitsa). UnsolvedMath
record UM-00701518 (`KOU-18.89`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

`ACₙ` is the subgroup of `Sym(Fₙⁿ)` generated by the elementary moves on relator tuples `uᵢ ↦ uᵢ
uⱼ^{±1}`, `uᵢ ↦ uⱼ^{±1} uᵢ` (`i ≠ j`), `uᵢ ↦ uᵢ⁻¹`, `uᵢ ↦ w uᵢ w⁻¹` (`w ∈ Fₙ`): Roman'kov's general
Andrews-Curtis group `GACₙ(Fₙ)`, which he shows isomorphic to the Andrews-Curtis group `AC(Fₙ)` on
normally generating tuples. The answer depends on `n`: one target for `n = 2` and one for all `n ≥
3` (the groups are considered for `n ≥ 2`).
-/

/-- `f` is an elementary Andrews–Curtis move on `n`-tuples `(u₁, …, uₙ)` of elements of the free
group `Fₙ`: `uᵢ ↦ uᵢ uⱼ^{±1}` or `uᵢ ↦ uⱼ^{±1} uᵢ` (`i ≠ j`), `uᵢ ↦ uᵢ⁻¹`, or `uᵢ ↦ w uᵢ w⁻¹`
(`w ∈ Fₙ`), all other entries unchanged. -/
def IsACMove (n : ℕ) (f : (Fin n → FreeGroup (Fin n)) → (Fin n → FreeGroup (Fin n))) : Prop :=
  (∃ i j : Fin n, i ≠ j ∧
    (f = (fun u => Function.update u i (u i * u j)) ∨
      f = (fun u => Function.update u i (u i * (u j)⁻¹)) ∨
      f = (fun u => Function.update u i (u j * u i)) ∨
      f = (fun u => Function.update u i ((u j)⁻¹ * u i)))) ∨
  (∃ i : Fin n, f = fun u => Function.update u i (u i)⁻¹) ∨
  (∃ (i : Fin n) (w : FreeGroup (Fin n)), f = fun u => Function.update u i (w * u i * w⁻¹))

/-- The group `ACₙ` of Andrews–Curtis moves on balanced presentations `⟨x₁, …, xₙ | u₁, …, uₙ⟩`:
the subgroup of `Sym(Fₙⁿ)` generated by the elementary moves (Nielsen transformations of the relator
tuple together with conjugations of relators). -/
def andrewsCurtisGroup (n : ℕ) : Subgroup (Equiv.Perm (Fin n → FreeGroup (Fin n))) :=
  Subgroup.closure {σ | IsACMove n σ}

/-- Kourovka 18.89 (J. Swan, A. Lisitsa) for two generators: `AC₂` is finitely presented. -/
def andrewsCurtisTwoFinitelyPresented : Prop :=
  Group.IsFinitelyPresented (andrewsCurtisGroup 2)

/-- Kourovka 18.89 for at least three generators: `ACₙ` is finitely presented for every `n ≥ 3`. -/
def andrewsCurtisFinitelyPresentedOfThreeLe : Prop :=
  ∀ n : ℕ, 3 ≤ n → Group.IsFinitelyPresented (andrewsCurtisGroup n)

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) records that `AC₂` is not finitely presented
(Krstić-McCool, 1997; Roman'kov, 2023, who also shows that the group of moves on all relator tuples
is isomorphic to the Andrews-Curtis group `AC(F₂) ≅ A₂,₂`). Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov
(eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49
(2026), Problem 18.89 (J. Swan, A. Lisitsa); resolution: S. Krstić and J. McCool, J. London Math.
Soc. (2) 56 (1997), no. 2, 264-274; V. A. Roman'kov, On the Andrews-Curtis groups: non-finite
presentability, preprint, arXiv:2305.11838 (2023); G. Kiralis, S. Krstić and J. McCool, Proc. London
Math. Soc. (3) 73 (1996), no. 3, 575-622; M. Ershov, letter of 16 October 2025, AC_2 is not finitely
presented (Krstić-McCool, Theorem 1; Roman'kov, Theorem B: AC(F_2) ≅ A_{2,2} is not finitely
presented); AC_n, n ≥ 3, is finitely presented by Kiralis-Krstić-McCool, Theorem 3.1, since AC_n ≅
A_{n,n} ≅ Φ_n(F_n) (Ershov's letter); the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 18.89 solved,
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/kourovka1889.pdf.
-/
public theorem_wanted andrewsCurtisTwoFinitelyPresented_refuted :
    ¬ andrewsCurtisTwoFinitelyPresented

/--
Resolved true: the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) records that `ACₙ` is finitely presented for `n ≥
3`, which follows from Kiralis-Krstić-McCool (1996), Theorem 3.1 (`Φₙ(G)` is finitely presented for
`n ≥ 3` when `G` is), since `ACₙ ≅ Aₙ,ₙ ≅ Φₙ(Fₙ)` (M. Ershov, letter of 2025, using Roman'kov's
Lemma 3.2). Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The
Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Problem 18.89 (J. Swan, A. Lisitsa);
resolution: S. Krstić and J. McCool, J. London Math. Soc. (2) 56 (1997), no. 2, 264-274; V. A.
Roman'kov, On the Andrews-Curtis groups: non-finite presentability, preprint, arXiv:2305.11838
(2023); G. Kiralis, S. Krstić and J. McCool, Proc. London Math. Soc. (3) 73 (1996), no. 3, 575-622;
M. Ershov, letter of 16 October 2025, AC_2 is not finitely presented (Krstić-McCool, Theorem 1;
Roman'kov, Theorem B: AC(F_2) ≅ A_{2,2} is not finitely presented); AC_n, n ≥ 3, is finitely
presented by Kiralis-Krstić-McCool, Theorem 3.1, since AC_n ≅ A_{n,n} ≅ Φ_n(F_n) (Ershov's letter);
the Kourovka Notebook (21st issue) marks 18.89 solved,
https://kourovkanotebookorg.wordpress.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/kourovka1889.pdf.
-/
public theorem_wanted andrewsCurtisFinitelyPresentedOfThreeLe_holds :
    andrewsCurtisFinitelyPresentedOfThreeLe

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.AndrewsCurtisGroupPresentationKourovka18Problem89Wanted
